// ============================================================================
// Project 25 (Meridian Pay) -- Session 3, Phase 2
// 06_gate_recheck_queries.kql
//
// Queries to re-check gates C2 and C3 now that the KQL layer exists and the
// backfill has (or is about to have) loaded. Both gates were left as findings,
// not passes, at the end of Session 2 (Gate_Check_Results.md) specifically
// because EH_MeridianPay had zero tables at that point.
// ============================================================================

// ---- C2: Direct Lake via OneLake availability ------------------------
// Session 2's finding: OneLake availability needs >=1 KQL table to exist
// before it can even be exercised (confirmed by the portal). That is now
// true -- 12 tables exist after 00-03 run. Two things still have to happen,
// and BOTH are portal actions, not KQL:
//   1. Per table (start with dim_estate and auth_curated -- the two the
//      Direct Lake model will actually bind to), toggle "OneLake availability"
//      ON in the Eventhouse UI (table right-click -> OneLake availability, or
//      the database-level "Enable OneLake availability" toggle for all
//      tables at once). This starts the Delta mirror.
//   2. Wait for the mirror to catch up (materialization lag, typically under
//      a few minutes for tables this size), then in a NEW semantic model
//      (Power BI service, "New report" -> "Get data" -> "Fabric Eventhouse")
//      confirm the Eventhouse's tables are offered as a Direct Lake source.
//
// This KQL query does not toggle OneLake availability (that is portal-only,
// no management command exposes it as of this engine's last confirmed check)
// -- it only confirms mirroring has actually caught up once the toggle is on,
// which the earlier finding could not verify because there was nothing to
// mirror yet. If '.show table <T> details' does not include OneLake/mirroring
// fields on this cluster, treat that as informational, not a KQL API gap --
// read the mirroring status from the portal (Eventhouse -> table -> OneLake
// availability column) instead, and note in Gate_Check_Results.md which
// route actually worked.
.show table dim_estate details
.show table auth_curated details

// ---- C3: Eventhouse idle CU burn, trend not a single reading -----------
// This gate cannot be exercised in KQL at all -- it is read from the tenant's
// Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app (Workspaces -> "Microsoft Fabric
// Ca..." -> Compute page -> Items (14 days) table), per Session 2's method.
// What changed since Session 2: EH_MeridianPay now has real ingested data
// (~9.7m rows) and real query load (every function/MV/DQ-rule query above),
// so this re-check should show non-zero CU for EH_MeridianPay specifically,
// not just for the unrelated Order Management workspace.
//
// Steps (portal, written not automated -- CLAUDE.md cheapest-route rule; the
// one browser-automation exception in Session 2 was scoped to that gate only
// and does not carry forward as a standing approval):
//   1. Open the Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app.
//   2. Compute page -> filter to Trial-20250909T024501Z-DE5kgPHwekiZZR...
//      (the user's own trial, confirmed in Session 2's C0 finding).
//   3. Items (14 days) table -> find EH_MeridianPay and ACT_MeridianPay rows.
//      If either is still absent, wait for the next metrics refresh (these
//      typically lag by up to a few hours) and check again -- do not report
//      absence as a pass on a single reading, per the contract's own rule
//      that a gate re-read once is a finding, not a pass.
//   4. Record the CU(s) reading AND its refresh timestamp in
//      Gate_Check_Results.md's Session 3 section, plus a comparison against
//      Session 2's shared-capacity baseline (Order Management, ~2.35% avg
//      utilization / ~1.76M CU-seconds over 14 days) -- the question C3 is
//      actually answering is whether this project's own load fits comfortably
//      alongside that baseline, which needs two points on the trend, not one.
